          "text": "647.  The  Penja,  Teli,  and  Qassab  (Castes  Nos.  83,  23  and  38).— The \nPenja,  as  often  called  Pumba  or  Dhunia,  and  in  the  cities  Naddaf,  is  the \ncotton  scutcher  who,  striking  a  bow  with  a  heavy  wooden  plectmm,  uses  the \nvibrations  of  the  bow-string  to  separate  the  filu'cs  of  the  cotton,  to  arrange \nthem  side  by  side,  and  to  part  them  fi'om  dirt  and  other  imi^m*ities.  The  Teli \nis  the  oil-presser ;  and  the  Qassab  the  butcher  who  slaughters  after  the \nMahomedan  fashion,  dresses  the  carcase,  and  sells  the  meat.  But  while  the \nTeli  appears  to  be  a  true  caste,  the  Qassab  and  Penja  are  only  names  of  occu- \npations which  are  almost  invariably  followed  by  Telis.  In  Mnlttin  and  the  [?•  334] \nD(Tajut  the  Teli  is  commonly  called  Chaki  or  Chakani,  and  a  quaint  story  con- \ncerning hin\\  is  related  by  Mr.  O^Brien  at  page  93  of  his  Multdni  Glossary.  The \n\nTeli;  including  the  Penja  and  Qassab,  is  very  uniformly  distributed  over  the \nProvince  save  in  the  hills  proper,  where  oil  and  cotton  are  imported  and  the \nHindu  population  need  no  butchers.  He  is  naturally  most  numerous  in  great \ncities,  while  on  the  frontier  he  is,  like  most  occupational  castes,  less  common. \nIn  the  Derajat,  however^  many  of  the  Qassabs  would  appear  to  have  been  re- \nturned as  Jats  (see  Abstract  No.  72,  page  224t).  t  P- 106- \n\nThe  numbers  returned  for  Gurgjion  under  the  head  Qassab  seem  extra- \nordinarily large  ;  but  I  can  detect  no  error  in  the  tables.'  The  Teli  is  almost \nexclusively  a  Musalmiin  ;  and  the  Hindu  Penja  of  the  eastern  districts  is  said \nto  be  known  by  the  name  of  Kandera,  a  word,  however,  which  appears  to  be \napplied  to  Musalm^n  Pcnjas  also  in  Rajputana. "
